@endnode

@node Partition_names "Partition names"
@{b}Partition names@{ub}

@{b}RDB@{ub} partitions keep their on-disk name ( @{fg shine}pb_DriveName@{fg text} ) verbatim, for
example @{fg shine}DH0@{fg text} .

@{b}MBR, GPT and flat@{ub} partitions carry no on-disk name, so the library
synthesizes one: @{fg shine}PREFIX@{fg text} + unit-letter + partition-number.

  * @{b}PREFIX:@{ub} a short device abbreviation from a built-in table, or, for devices not
    in the table, the device's base name with @{fg shine}.device@{fg text} stripped and the @{fg shine}A-Z@{fg text} and @{fg shine}0-9@{fg text}
    characters uppercased. Currently only @{fg shine}compactflash.device@{fg text} has an abbreviation
    ( @{fg shine}CF@{fg text} ); everything else falls back to the base name.
  * @{b}unit-letter:@{ub} lowercase @{fg shine}a@{fg text} + unit (@{fg shine}a@{fg text} = unit 0, @{fg shine}b@{fg text} = unit 1, up to @{fg shine}p@{fg text} = unit 15).
  * @{b}partition-number:@{ub} 0-based decimal.

  @{b}Device@{ub}                     @{b}Unit@{ub}  @{b}Synthesized names@{ub}  
  -------------------------  ----  -----------------  
  @{fg shine}compactflash.device@{fg text}        0     @{fg shine}CFa0@{fg text}, @{fg shine}CFa1@{fg text}, @{fg shine}CFa2@{fg text}   
  (abbrev @{fg shine}CF@{fg text} )                                        
  @{fg shine}scsi.device@{fg text} (base name to  0     @{fg shine}SCSIa0@{fg text}, @{fg shine}SCSIa1@{fg text}     
  @{fg shine}SCSI@{fg text} )                                              
  @{fg shine}scsi.device@{fg text}                2     @{fg shine}SCSIc0@{fg text}, @{fg shine}SCSIc1@{fg text}     
  @{fg shine}mfm.device@{fg text} (base name to   1     @{fg shine}MFMb0@{fg text}, @{fg shine}MFMb1@{fg text}       
  @{fg shine}MFM@{fg text} )                                               

To give a device its own abbreviation instead of the base-name fallback,
add it to @{fg shine}s_devAbbrevTable@{fg text} in @{fg shine}../src/ptable_scan.s@{fg text}
(../src/ptable_scan.s).

@{b}Two names, @{fg shine}scanname>dosname@{fg text} .@{ub} @{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text} shows both names when the DOS
device name differs from the scan name, and there are two ways that
happens:

  * You mount the partition yourself from a static @{fg shine}DEVS:DOSDrivers@{fg text} entry. The
    handler registers its real DOS name, plus the Flags and CONTROL it opened the
    device with, back onto the published entry, so @{fg shine}CFa0>MS0@{fg text} is the partition
    scanned as @{fg shine}CFa0@{fg text} and mounted as @{fg shine}MS0:@{fg text} . This is the same @{fg shine}RegisterPartition@{fg text} path
    cfd's automount uses, so the @{fg shine}MFlg@{fg text} and @{fg shine}Ctrl@{fg text} columns are accurate whichever way
    a partition was mounted.
  * The name clashed with an existing mount and was uniquified at register time.
    Two cards whose RDBs both define @{fg shine}DH0@{fg text} give @{fg shine}DH0@{fg text} and @{fg shine}DH0>DH0.1@{fg text} .

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Flows}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Configuration}

@endnode

@node Configuration "Configuration"
@{b}Configuration@{ub}

@{fg shine}ptable.library@{fg text} has no preferences file of its own. Its behaviour comes
from two places.

@{b}1. On-disk RDB metadata (cold-boot path).@{ub} Each RDB partition carries its
own flags, and the library uses them as-is:

  @{b}RDB partition flag@{ub}    @{b}Result@{ub}                                  
  --------------------  --------------------------------------  
  bootable (bit 0 set)  appears in the Early Startup boot list  
  bootable clear        mounted as a DOS volume, not offered    
                        as a boot device                        
  NOMOUNT (bit 1 set)   published but never mounted             

BootPri, stored in the partition environment, does not decide
bootability; it orders the boot list. To change which partition boots,
or whether one mounts, edit the RDB with a partitioning tool such as
HDToolBox.

@{b}2. The consumer's mount configuration ( @{fg shine}MountCfg@{fg text} , DOS-time path).@{ub}
@{fg shine}MountPartitions@{fg text} takes an optional @{fg shine}MountCfg@{fg text} supplying a global mount
@{fg shine}Flags@{fg text} value, a global @{fg shine}CONTROL@{fg text} string, and a per-filesystem override
table. The library resolves each partition's @{fg shine}Flags@{fg text} and @{fg shine}CONTROL@{fg text} by its
DosType (matching the high three bytes, e.g. @{fg shine}FAT\0@{fg text} ), falling back to
the global value. Passing @{fg shine}0@{fg text} selects cold-boot defaults.

For @{fg shine}compactflash.device@{fg text} these values come from @{fg shine}ENV:cfd.prefs@{fg text} : the @{fg shine}FLAGS@{fg text}
and @{fg shine}CONTROL@{fg text} keys, global and @{fg shine}_<fs>@{fg text} per filesystem. So to change how
hotplugged cards mount, you edit @{fg shine}cfd.prefs@{fg text} , not @{fg shine}ptable.library@{fg text} . The
resolved values are recorded per partition and shown live by @{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text} in
its @{fg shine}MFlg@{fg text} and @{fg shine}Ctrl@{fg text} columns.

The full user-facing reference for the @{fg shine}cfd.prefs@{fg text} keys, plus deployment
defaults and the removable-media model, is @{fg shine}compactflash.device@{fg text} 's
automount guide ( @{fg shine}automount.guide@{fg text} ).

@{b}Detach policy (card removal).@{ub} Two ways to handle a removed card's
partitions:

  * @{b}Keep, @{fg shine}MarkAbsent@{fg text} :@{ub} clear @{fg shine}PRESENT@{fg text} but keep the DOS node and handler in memory.
    The entry stays listed as absent-but-mounted ( @{fg shine}---M@{fg text} ), and reinserting the
    same card reattaches it without re-initialising the handler. This is the
    native AmigaOS removable-media model. @{fg shine}compactflash.device@{fg text} takes this path when
    the @{fg shine}UNMOUNT@{fg text} key lists no recognised filesystem, @{fg shine}UNMOUNT NONE@{fg text} being the usual
    spelling.
  * @{b}Tear down, @{fg shine}UnmountPartitions@{fg text} with a prefix list:@{ub} stop the handler, remove its
    DOS node (ACTION_DIE + RemDosEntry + free the node) and drop the entry from
    the resource. Only filesystems in the list are torn down; any other matched
    entry is kept and marked absent, exactly as @{fg shine}MarkAbsent@{fg text} would leave it. With no
    prefix list every mounted entry for the device and unit is torn down.
    @{fg shine}compactflash.device@{fg text} passes all supported filesystems by default, and an
    explicit @{fg shine}UNMOUNT@{fg text} key restricts it, for example @{fg shine}UNMOUNT FAT@{fg text} .

@{b}When an unmount does not happen.@{ub} @{fg shine}UnmountPartitions@{fg text} keeps the mount and
only marks the partition absent if the handler is still alive three
seconds after @{fg shine}ACTION_DIE@{fg text} . Nothing is freed in that case: the DOS node
and the handler are left as they were, and the next card removal tries
again. A volume that something still holds a lock on is the ordinary
reason, because a filesystem cannot give up a volume whose node has to
stay in the DOS list for that lock to remain valid. Expect
@{fg shine}UnmountPartitions@{fg text} to return fewer entries than were mounted, and the
resource to still hold @{fg shine}---M@{fg text} rows afterwards.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Partition_names}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link What_it_looks_like__serial_debug_}

@endnode

@node What_it_looks_like__serial_debug_ "What it looks like (serial debug)"
@{b}What it looks like (serial debug)@{ub}

The @{fg shine}full@{fg text} build prints its progress to the serial port at 9600 baud, the
@{fg shine}small@{fg text} build is silent. The library tags its own lines @{fg shine}[PT]@{fg text} . In the
traces below the surrounding @{fg shine}[CFD] boot:@{fg text} and @{fg shine}[MW]@{fg text} lines come from
@{fg shine}compactflash.device@{fg text} and show where the library was called from.

@{b}Cold boot, RDB autoboot.@{ub} The cold stub opens the library and calls
@{fg shine}BootScanPartitions@{fg text} :

@{fg highlight}
  [CFD] boot: open ptable.library ...
  [CFD] boot: ptable.library not preloaded, InitResident()...
  [CFD] boot: BootScanPartitions(compactflash.device,0)
  [PT] cold boot: scanning for partitions
  [PT] RDB partition table
  [PT] + filesystem handler PFS v20.0
  [PT] new partitions: 5
  [PT] - skip  SDH10 (no-mount)
  [PT] - skip  SDH11 (no-mount)
  [PT] + boot  SDH0 (PFS., 512 MB)
  [PT] + mount SDH1 (PFS., 4096 MB)
  [PT] + mount SDH2 (PFS., 21767 MB)
  [PT] cold boot done, partitions registered: 3
@{fg text}

Five partitions are published, the two NOMOUNT entries are skipped, one
bootable is registered ( @{fg shine}+ boot@{fg text} ) and two mountable ( @{fg shine}+ mount@{fg text} ). The
DosType prints as four characters with @{fg shine}.@{fg text} standing in for a NUL byte, so
@{fg shine}PFS\0@{fg text} reads @{fg shine}PFS.@{fg text} .

A FAT card at cold boot is published and left for DOS time, which is
what the other skip reason means:

@{fg highlight}
  [PT] cold boot: scanning for partitions
  [PT] GPT partition table
  [PT] new partitions: 1
  [PT] - skip  CFa0 (not RDB: mounted at DOS time)
  [PT] cold boot done, partitions registered: 0
@{fg text}

@{b}Hotplug, DOS-time scan.@{ub} An inserted card is scanned, published, then
mounted by the consumer's mount worker. Here a GPT card with three FAT
partitions:

@{fg highlight}
  [MW] scanning compactflash.device:0 for partitions
  [PT] scanning for partitions
  [PT] GPT partition table
  [PT] new partitions: 3
  [MW] scan done, new partitions: 3
  [MW] mounting new partitions
  [PT] mounting partitions
  [PT] mounted CFa0 (FAT., 2048 MB)
  [PT] mounted CFa1 (FAT., 4096 MB)
  [PT] mounted CFa2 (FAT., 8192 MB)
  [MW] mounted volumes: 3
@{fg text}

@{fg shine}[PT] reusing handler <name>@{fg text} appears in place of @{fg shine}mounted@{fg text} when a DOS node
of that name already exists and is rebound instead of created, and
@{fg shine}[PT] mounted as <name>@{fg text} when a handler overlays its own DOS name through
@{fg shine}RegisterPartition@{fg text} . A whole-disk card prints
@{fg shine}[PT] whole-disk FAT (superfloppy)@{fg text} in place of a partition-table line.

The resulting @{fg shine}partition.resource@{fg text} , listed by @{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text} (columns explained
in @{"  __PAIRED_2__  " link "lsptres.guide/Main"} ):

@{fg highlight}
  Name         Device        Unit Part Src Pri DosType    Text Flags MFlg Ctrl
  ------------ ------------- ---- ---- --- --- ---------- ---- ----- ----- ----------
  CFa0         compactflash.    0    0 GPT   0 0x46415400 FAT. P--M      0 -d-D
  CFa1         compactflash.    0    1 GPT   0 0x46415400 FAT. P--M      0 -d-D
  CFa2         compactflash.    0    2 GPT   0 0x46415400 FAT. P--M      0 -d-D
@{fg text}

All three are mounted ( @{fg shine}P--M@{fg text} ), with the @{fg shine}Ctrl@{fg text} value @{fg shine}-d-D@{fg text} resolved from
@{fg shine}CONTROL_FAT@{fg text} in @{fg shine}cfd.prefs@{fg text} .

@{b}Card removed, keep policy.@{ub} With @{fg shine}UNMOUNT NONE@{fg text} the handlers stay in memory
and the entries are only marked absent, so reinserting the same card
reattaches them:

@{fg highlight}
  [MW] card removed
  [PT] card removed, media absent
  [MW] entries detached: 0
@{fg text}

@{fg highlight}
  CFa0         compactflash.    0    0 GPT   0 0x46415400 FAT. ---M      0 -d-D
  CFa1         compactflash.    0    1 GPT   0 0x46415400 FAT. ---M      0 -d-D
  CFa2         compactflash.    0    2 GPT   0 0x46415400 FAT. ---M      0 -d-D
@{fg text}

@{b}Card removed, teardown policy.@{ub} With the default list, or @{fg shine}UNMOUNT FAT@{fg text}
here, the FAT partitions are unmounted and dropped from the resource:

@{fg highlight}
  [MW] card removed
  [PT] unmounting partitions
  [PT] unmounted CFa0
  [PT] unmounted CFa1
  [PT] unmounted CFa2
  [MW] entries detached: 3
@{fg text}

A handler that will not go prints
@{fg shine}[PT] handler still alive after ACTION_DIE, kept absent@{fg text} , and that
partition stays @{fg shine}---M@{fg text} .

----------------------------------------
