@endnode

@node Public_interface_for_developers "Public interface for developers"
@{b}Public interface for developers@{ub}

@{fg shine}OpenLibrary("ptable.library", 2)@{fg text} . The tree builds one version, 2.0, and
every shipping consumer opens it at version 2 on every path, cold stub
included.

@{fg highlight}
  BootScanPartitions(deviceName:a1, unit:d0)                 -30
  ScanPartitions(deviceName:a1, unit:d0)                     -36
  MountPartitions(deviceName:a1, unit:d0, cfg:a0)            -42
  UnmountPartitions(deviceName:a1, unit:d0, prefixList:a0)   -48
  RegisterPartition(deviceName:a1, unit:d0, startLBA:d1, blockCount:d2,
                    nameBSTR:a0, devNode:a2, flags:d3, control:d4)  -54
  MarkAbsent(deviceName:a1, unit:d0)                         -60
@{fg text}

  @{b}LVO@{ub}                 @{b}Returns in d0@{ub}           @{b}Call from@{ub}                 
  ------------------  ----------------------  ------------------------  
  @{fg shine}BootScanPartitions@{fg text}  partitions registered   @{fg shine}RTF_COLDSTART@{fg text} , pre-DOS,  
                                              single task               
  @{fg shine}ScanPartitions@{fg text}      partitions @{b}newly@{ub}        any task, Exec context    
                      published                                         
  @{fg shine}MountPartitions@{fg text}     partitions mounted      a process                 
  @{fg shine}UnmountPartitions@{fg text}   entries torn down       a process                 
  @{fg shine}RegisterPartition@{fg text}   1 updated, 0 not found  any task, Exec context    
  @{fg shine}MarkAbsent@{fg text}          entries cleared         any task, Exec context    

Notes a consumer needs:

  * @{fg shine}MarkAbsent@{fg text} never blocks: it takes the resource lock with an attempt and does
    nothing at all if the lock is busy, so a return of 0 does not mean there were
    no entries.
  * @{fg shine}RegisterPartition@{fg text} matches on device, unit and start block. @{fg shine}control@{fg text} is a BSTR
    pointer, @{fg shine}0@{fg text} for none.
  * @{fg shine}cfg@{fg text} is a @{fg shine}MountCfg@{fg text} (or @{fg shine}0@{fg text} for cold-boot defaults): global @{fg shine}mc_Flags@{fg text} and
    @{fg shine}mc_Control@{fg text} plus a 0-terminated per-dostype override table, resolved by
    @{fg shine}(pe_DosType & $FFFFFF00)@{fg text} .
  * @{fg shine}prefixList@{fg text} is a 0-terminated list of dostype high three bytes, e.g. @{fg shine}$50465300@{fg text}
    for @{fg shine}PFS@{fg text} .
  * @{fg shine}BootScanPartitions@{fg text} also registers a synthetic ConfigDev (Vendor ID @{fg shine}65535@{fg text} ,
    Product ID @{fg shine}1@{fg text} ) when it registered at least one node, which is what puts the
    device in the Early Startup boot menu and in @{fg shine}ShowConfig@{fg text} .
  * For MBR, GPT and flat partitions the mount path builds an auto-detect
    DeviceNode: @{fg shine}de_LowCyl = 0@{fg text} and the device-scheme DosType @{fg shine}$464154FF@{fg text} , so one
    persistent fat95 handler binds once and picks its partition from the trailing
    digit of the node name. That is how a single handler tracks any card layout
    across swaps.

@{b}@{fg shine}partition.resource@{fg text} layout.@{ub} Readers walk @{fg shine}ptr_PartList@{fg text} under @{fg shine}Forbid()@{fg text} or
take @{fg shine}ptr_Lock@{fg text} . @{fg shine}OpenResource@{fg text} cannot negotiate versions, so the only
runtime layout signal is the pair of stamps in the header, @{fg shine}ptr_Layout@{fg text}
(currently @{fg shine}PTR_LAYOUT_V = 2@{fg text} ) and @{fg shine}ptr_EntrySize@{fg text} , plus @{fg shine}pe_Length@{fg text} per
entry. Fields are appended only, never inserted, because @{fg shine}lsptres@{fg text} and
fat95 ship separately-built offset mirrors; a consumer that needs a
newer field checks the stamp and degrades if it is older. The full field
layout is in @{fg shine}../src/ptable_pub.i@{fg text} (../src/ptable_pub.i).

@{b}Lock order.@{ub} @{fg shine}ptr_Lock@{fg text} is the outer lock, DOS list locks are only ever
taken inside it, and a handler started by the library must not call back
into a ptable LVO while starting up.

@{b}Resident priorities@{ub} , for ROM integrators: @{fg shine}ptable.library@{fg text} at 22 and
@{fg shine}compactflash.device@{fg text} at 21, both @{fg shine}RTF_AUTOINIT@{fg text} and ahead of everything
that uses them, and the cold stub @{fg shine}compactflash.autoboot@{fg text} at -5. The stub
has to run after every ROM filesystem has registered in
@{fg shine}FileSystem.resource@{fg text} (fat95 registers at 0) or it binds no handler, and
well above @{fg shine}strap@{fg text} at -60, because @{fg shine}AddBootNode@{fg text} must happen before strap
runs.

